Over the next two days, the warm bath with the story brought strength back as well as the ice bath did. The plain warm bath fell behind. Same water, different expectation.`,
      build(s) {
        s.headline("Belief.", { x: 0.75, y: 1.55, w: 11.83, h: 1.75, size: 104, align: "right", color: "accent" });
        s.lead("In one trial, a warm bath that people were told would help matched an ice bath for getting strength back.", { x: 6.58, y: 3.75, w: 6.0, h: 1.6, size: 24, align: "right" });
        s.source("Broatch, Petersen & Bishop, Med Sci Sports Exerc, 2014", { x: 4.58, y: 5.95, w: 8.0, align: "right", refs: ["broatch2014"] });
      },
    },
    {
      role: "problem", time: 55,
      notes: `So here is the problem for all of us who pick recovery tools. We judge them by how athletes say they feel afterward. And how they feel is shaped by what they expect.

Two examples. Elite netball players rated contrast showers as better recovery than sitting down, but their agility the next day was the same. In rugby players, how highly they rated a cold bath went along with how well they sprinted the next day, on top of the cooling itself.

The feeling is real, and it matters. It just isn't all coming from the tool.`,
      build(s) {
        s.rect(8.9, 0, 4.433, 7.5, { fill: "soft" });
        s.headline("The feeling is real. It isn't all the tool.", { x: 0.75, y: 1.3, w: 7.45, h: 1.9, size: 48, align: "right" });
        s.lead("We judge recovery tools by how athletes feel. Expectation shapes that feeling.", { x: 1.7, y: 3.75, w: 6.5, h: 1.3, size: 24, align: "right" });
        s.label("Contrast showers", { x: 9.45, y: 1.5, w: 3.2 });
        s.text(9.45, 1.9, 3.2, 1.0, "Felt better. Agility the same.", { font: "light", size: 22, lineH: 1.15 });
        s.source("Juliff et al. 2014", { x: 9.45, y: 2.95, w: 3.2, h: 0.3, size: 12, valign: "top", refs: ["juliff2014"] });
        s.line(9.45, 3.75, 12.58, 3.75, { color: "rule", w: 1 });
        s.label("Cold baths, rugby", { x: 9.45, y: 4.1, w: 3.2 });
        s.text(9.45, 4.5, 3.2, 1.0, "Rating the bath went with sprinting well.", { font: "light", size: 22, lineH: 1.15 });
        s.source("Cook & Beaven 2013", { x: 9.45, y: 5.55, w: 3.2, h: 0.3, size: 12, valign: "top", refs: ["cook2013"] });
      },
    },
    {
      role: "insight", time: 65,
      notes: `Here is the way I'd picture it. How recovered an athlete feels is one bar. Part of it is what the tool actually does: there is good evidence that massage, compression and cold water ease soreness. The rest is what the athlete expects it to do.

The split you see is a picture, not a measurement. Nobody has a reliable percentage. But across sport, placebo effects on performance come out small to moderate, and the same review found the flip side: when people expect something to hurt their performance, it can.

So belief is part of the dose. It works with the tool, not instead of it.`,
      build(s) {
        s.headline("Belief is part of the dose.", { x: 0.75, y: 1.3, w: 11.83, h: 1.0, size: 56, align: "right" });
        s.lead("It works with the tool, not instead of it.", { x: 4.58, y: 2.45, w: 8.0, h: 0.7, size: 24, align: "right" });
        const x0 = 4.38, x1 = 12.58, split = 9.38, y = 4.55, h = 0.62;
        s.rect(x0, y, split - x0, h, { fill: "shape", opacity: 0.4 });
        s.rect(split, y, x1 - split, h, { fill: "accent" });
        s.line(x0, y - 0.18, x0, y + h + 0.18, { color: "muted", w: 1 });
        s.label("What the tool does", { x: x0, y: y - 0.55, w: 4.5 });
        s.label("What belief adds", { x: split, y: y - 0.55, w: 3.2, align: "right", color: "accent" });
        s.text(x0, y + h + 0.22, 8.2, 0.4, "How recovered an athlete feels", { font: "light", size: 18, color: "ink" });
        s.text(x0, y + h + 0.68, 8.2, 0.36, "Illustrative split, not data", { font: "light", size: 13, color: "muted" });
        s.source("Broatch et al. 2014 · Hurst et al. 2020 · Dupuy et al. 2018", { x: 4.58, w: 8.0, align: "right", refs: ["broatch2014", "hurst2020", "dupuy2018"] });
      },
    },
    {
      role: "application", time: 60,
      notes: `So what do we do on Monday? Keep the recovery tools athletes trust, as long as they're safe and they fit what we're training for. The tool and the belief together are doing something useful.

The part to watch is how we talk about them. Explain honestly what a tool does and doesn't do. But don't make a habit of telling athletes their routine is useless right before they compete. The review that found placebo effects found nocebo effects of about the same size: negative expectations can cost performance too.

And to be clear, none of this means handing athletes fake treatments.`,
      build(s) {
        s.headline("Keep the tools athletes trust, if they're safe and fit the goal.", { x: 1.6, y: 1.55, w: 10.98, h: 1.9, size: 44, align: "right" });
        s.line(9.58, 4.05, 12.58, 4.05, { color: "rule", w: 1 });
        s.lead("Explain them honestly. Talking a tool down can cost performance too.", { x: 5.08, y: 4.35, w: 7.5, h: 1.3, size: 26, align: "right", color: "ink" });
        s.source("Nocebo effects: Hurst et al. 2020", { x: 4.58, w: 8.0, align: "right", refs: ["hurst2020"] });
      },
    },
    {
      role: "takeaway", time: 55,
      notes: `The line to take away: belief is a recovery tool. Use it honestly.

That means the routine an athlete trusts deserves some respect, as long as it is safe and it fits the training goal. Deck ten is about the case where a trusted tool, cold after lifting, works against the goal.

Question for the room: which recovery routine do our athletes trust most, and does it fit the goal? Coaches and Athletic Medicine will often have different answers, and that conversation is the useful part.`,
      build(s) {
        s.headline("Belief is a recovery tool. Use it honestly.", { x: 0.75, y: 1.35, w: 7.4, h: 2.0, size: 56 });
        s.text(5.08, 4.15, 7.5, 1.6, "Which recovery routine do our athletes trust most, and does it fit the goal?", { font: "medium", size: 30, color: "accent", lineH: 1.15, align: "right" });
      },
    },
  ],
};

