// 09 · Active Recovery vs. Complete Rest: sort the kind of tired first, then move or stop.
// Composition: hook = one question, nothing else; takeaway = centered line with the question
// beneath; alignment = center (insight and takeaway centered).

module.exports = {
  num: 9,
  slug: "active-recovery-vs-complete-rest",
  title: "Active Recovery vs. Complete Rest",
  category: "Recovery & Load Management",
  palette: "charcoal",
  hero: { layout: "decision-fork", concept: "One question (normal training fatigue, or something else?) splitting into two paths: move easy, or stop and bring in Athletic Medicine" },
  compose: { hook: "question-only", takeaway: "centered", align: "center" },
  oneIdea: "Sort the kind of tired first. Normal training fatigue: easy movement or rest are both fine. Pain, illness or a head knock: stop and let Athletic Medicine set the plan.",
  question: "Who makes the move-or-stop call for our athletes now, and does everyone know it?",
  caveats: `The evidence on active recovery is genuinely mixed. Van Hooren & Peake's review finds an active cool-down largely ineffective for next-day performance and most recovery markers, while Dupuy et al.'s meta-analysis finds active recovery reduced soreness; the studies, outcomes and timing differ. The honest summary is "a small benefit at most, and no harm," not "proven." The fork is a sorting question, not a medical rule. Anything involving pain, illness, fever, a head injury or a new injury belongs to Athletic Medicine; this deck does not tell anyone how to manage those. The concussion trial (Thomas et al.) was adolescents and young adults seen in a pediatric emergency department, and it is mentioned only to show that "total rest" is not automatically the safe choice even there. Return-to-play decisions are Athletic Medicine's.`,
  sources: [
    { id: "vanhooren2018", cite: "Van Hooren B, Peake JM. Do we need a cool-down after exercise? A narrative review of the psychophysiological effects and the effects on performance, injuries and the long-term adaptive response. Sports Med. 2018;48(7):1575-1595. doi:10.1007/s40279-018-0916-2", grade: "narrative review", found: "external (cited across Zotero items, e.g. Haugen 2019, Düking 2024)", supports: "An active cool-down speeds blood lactate removal but is largely ineffective for same-day and next-day performance, most recovery markers and muscle soreness; it does not appear to blunt long-term adaptation." },
    { id: "dupuy2018", cite: "Dupuy O, Douzi W, Theurot D, Bosquet L, Dugué B. An evidence-based approach for choosing post-exercise recovery techniques to reduce markers of muscle damage, soreness, fatigue, and inflammation: a systematic review with meta-analysis. Front Physiol. 2018;9:403. doi:10.3389/fphys.2018.00403", grade: "systematic review and meta-analysis (99 studies)", found: "Zotero", supports: "Active recovery was among the techniques that reduced delayed-onset muscle soreness; the disagreeing result that makes the evidence mixed." },
    { id: "thomas2015", cite: "Thomas DG, Apps JN, Hoffmann RG, McCrea M, Hammeke T. Benefits of strict rest after acute concussion: a randomized controlled trial. Pediatrics. 2015;135(2):213-223. doi:10.1542/peds.2014-0966", grade: "randomized controlled trial (n=88, ages 11-22, pediatric emergency department)", found: "external", supports: "Five days of strict rest gave no added benefit over usual care (1-2 days of rest, then a stepwise return) and was linked to more reported symptoms." },
  ],
  slides: [
    {
      role: "hook", time: 40,
      notes: `Here is a question every one of us gets asked, or answers by habit, all season. An athlete is sore and tired the day after a hard session. Do they move, or do they rest?

Some of us were raised on "get the blood flowing." Some of us were raised on "shut it down." Today is about which one is right, and the honest answer is that it depends on a different question.`,
      build(s) {
        s.headline("Sore and tired: move, or rest?", { x: 0.75, y: 2.2, w: 11.83, h: 2.6, size: 88, align: "center" });
      },
    },
    {
      role: "problem", time: 65,
      notes: `Here is what the research says about the "move" habit. Easy movement after training does clear lactate from the blood faster. That part is solid.

But when researchers look at what we actually care about, next-day performance and how sore people are, the results are small or mixed. One large review found an active cool-down mostly doesn't change those things. A meta-analysis found active recovery did reduce soreness somewhat. Different studies, different answers.

So neither habit wins every time. Easy movement is fine and does no harm. It just isn't the whole decision.`,
      build(s) {
        s.headline("Neither habit is right every time.", { x: 2.17, y: 1.5, w: 9.0, h: 1.0, size: 52, align: "center" });
        s.lead("Easy movement clears blood lactate faster. Its effect on next-day soreness and performance is small or mixed.", { x: 2.67, y: 3.1, w: 8.0, h: 1.9, size: 28, align: "center", color: "ink" });
        s.source("Van Hooren & Peake 2018 · Dupuy et al. 2018", { x: 2.67, y: 5.6, w: 8.0, align: "center", refs: ["vanhooren2018", "dupuy2018"] });
      },
    },
    {
      role: "insight", time: 70,
      notes: `So the decision starts somewhere else. Ask what kind of tired it is.

If it's normal training fatigue, the sore legs and heavy feeling you'd expect after hard work, then move or rest, whichever the athlete prefers. Easy movement won't hurt and may help a little.

If it's something else, pain that's sharp or getting worse, a fever, a knock to the head, anything new, then stop and bring in Athletic Medicine. They set the plan from there. And even then, total rest isn't automatically the safe answer: in one concussion trial, young patients told to rest strictly for five days reported more symptoms than those who rested a day or two and then built back up. That's Athletic Medicine's call, not ours.`,
      build(s) {
        s.headline("Ask what kind of tired it is.", { x: 0.75, y: 1.25, w: 11.83, h: 1.0, size: 56, align: "center" });
        const cx = 6.667, top = 3.55, knee = 4.05, ly = 4.95, lx = 3.67, rx = 9.67;
        s.text(2.67, 2.75, 8.0, 0.5, "Normal training fatigue, or something else?", { font: "light", size: 22, align: "center" });
        s.line(cx, top, cx, knee, { color: "muted", w: 2 });
        s.line(cx, knee, lx, ly, { color: "muted", w: 2 });
        s.line(cx, knee, rx, ly, { color: "accent", w: 3 });
        s.circle(lx, ly, 0.09, { fill: "muted" });
        s.circle(rx, ly, 0.11, { fill: "accent" });
        s.text(lx - 2.4, ly + 0.2, 4.8, 0.5, "Move or rest", { font: "condBold", size: 26, align: "center" });
        s.text(lx - 2.4, ly + 0.68, 4.8, 0.4, "Normal soreness and fatigue", { font: "light", size: 18, color: "muted", align: "center" });
        s.text(rx - 2.6, ly + 0.2, 5.2, 0.5, "Stop", { font: "condBold", size: 26, color: "accent", align: "center" });
        s.text(rx - 2.6, ly + 0.68, 5.2, 0.4, "Pain, illness, a head knock: Athletic Medicine", { font: "light", size: 18, color: "muted", align: "center" });
        s.cite("vanhooren2018", "thomas2015");
      },
    },
    {
      role: "application", time: 60,
      notes: `Here is how that looks on a normal week. Two rows.

When an athlete is sore and tired after normal work, let them choose: an easy walk, a bike, a swim, or simply rest. Don't force a recovery session on someone who'd rather rest, and don't stop someone who feels better moving.

When there's pain, a fever, a head knock or anything that's new, they stop, and Athletic Medicine hears about it the same day. Coaches, Athletic Performance, academics: if an athlete mentions one of those to you, that's the handoff.`,
      build(s) {
        s.headline("Sort first, then choose.", { x: 0.75, y: 1.2, w: 11.83, h: 0.9, size: 44, align: "center" });
        const L = 1.67, mid = 5.9, R = 11.67;
        s.label("When", { x: L, y: 2.5, w: 3.0 });
        s.label("Do", { x: mid, y: 2.5, w: 3.0 });
        [["Sore and tired after normal work", "Easy movement or rest. Let the athlete choose."], ["Pain, fever, a head knock, or anything new", "Stop. Tell Athletic Medicine the same day."]].forEach(([w, d], i) => {
          const y = 2.95 + i * 1.5;
          s.line(L, y, R, y, { color: "rule", w: 1 });
          s.text(L, y + 0.22, 3.9, 1.1, w, { font: "light", size: 24, color: "muted", lineH: 1.15 });
          s.text(mid, y + 0.22, 5.77, 1.1, d, { font: "light", size: 26, lineH: 1.15 });
        });
        s.line(L, 5.95, R, 5.95, { color: "rule", w: 1 });
      },
    },
    {
      role: "takeaway", time: 50,
      notes: `The line to take away: move for fatigue, stop for anything else.

The sorting question is simple. What makes it work is that everyone knows who takes the handoff when the answer is "something else."

Question for the room: who makes the move-or-stop call for our athletes right now, and does everyone know it? Ask the coaches and Athletic Medicine to answer separately and see if they match.`,
      build(s) {
        s.headline("Move for fatigue.\nStop for anything else.", { x: 0.75, y: 1.45, w: 11.83, h: 2.4, size: 72, align: "center" });
        s.text(2.17, 4.4, 9.0, 1.2, "Who makes the move-or-stop call for our athletes now, and does everyone know it?", { font: "medium", size: 28, color: "accent", lineH: 1.15, align: "center" });
      },
    },
  ],
};

